      <example id="exam-00002">
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Which one appears to be the bounciest? Which one appears to be the least bouncy? Be prepared to show your
        reasoning. </para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00010">The second ball is the bounciest. The balls were all dropped from the same height, and its bounces are the highest.
    The third ball is the least bouncy. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example id="exam-00003">
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"> For each one, write an equation expressing the bounce height in terms of the bounce number \(n\). </para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answers:</para>
            <list id="list-00002">
              <item> Ball 1: \(a=150 \cdot (\frac{1}{2})^n\) or \(a=150 \cdot (0.5)^n\) </item>
              <item> Ball 2: \(b=150 \cdot (\frac{7}{10})^n\) or \(b=150 \cdot (0.7)^n\) </item>
              <item> Ball 3: \(c=150 \cdot (\frac{2}{5})^n\) or \(c=150 (0.4)^n\)</item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example id="exam-00004">
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014"> Explain how the equations could tell us which one is the most bouncy. </para>
            <para id="para-00015">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00016">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00017">The bounciness of the ball is measured by the rebound factor, which is the base of the exponential expression. That
    is \(\frac12\) for the first ball, \(\frac{7}{10}\) for the second ball, and \(\frac25\) for the third ball. The
    larger this number, the bouncier the ball.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example id="exam-00005">
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00018"> If the bounciest one were dropped from a height of 300 cm, what equation would model its bounce height \(h\)?
      </para>
            <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00021"> \(h=300 \cdot (\frac{7}{10})^n\) </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--START QUESTION # -->
      <example id="exam-00006">
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00022">Graph, on the same coordinate plane, the equation for each ball that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces using the Desmos tool below. </para>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para id="para-00023">After you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00025">
              <figure id="fig-00002">
                <media alt="Graph of the equation that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 1 after 5 bounces.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_13_06_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
      <!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00026">7. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 1 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 5.5.
      Using the equation: 
      \(h=150 \cdot (0.50)^n\) and graphing \(h=150 \cdot (0.50)^5\) The graph shows the height to be 4.6875 after 5 bounces \((n=5)\).

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
    </para>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        \( \frac{1}{2}\) after each bounce. </item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 11. It should decrease by
        \( \frac{1}{2}\) for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce), it will decrease by a factor of about \( \frac{1}{2}\).
        The 5th bounce would be \(11 \cdot \frac{1}{2}=5.5\). </item>
            <item>4.6875 rounds to 5, and 5.5 rounds to 6. </item>
            <item>Take the average of the two numbers.
        \(\frac{5+6}{2}=5.5\)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00030">8. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 2 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00031">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00033"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 25.
      Using the equation: 
      \(h=150 \cdot (0.7)^n\) and graphing \(h=150 \cdot (0.7)^5\). The graph shows the height to be 25.2105 after 5 bounces \((n=5)\).

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
    </para>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        \(\frac {7}{10}\) after each bounce. </item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 35. It should decrease by
        \(\frac {7}{10}\) for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce), it will
        decrease by a factor of about \(\frac {7}{10}\). The 5th bounce would be \(35 \cdot \frac {7}{10} \approx 24.5\). </item>
            <item>Rounding either 25.2105 or 24.5 results in the answer 25</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">9. Use the graph of the equation that models the bounce height \(h\) after \(n\) bounces for all 3 balls to predict the difference in the height of Ball 3 after 5 bounces.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00035">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037"> The difference in height after 5 bounces is 2.
      Using the equation: 
      \(h=150 \cdot (0.4)^n\) and graphing \(h=150 \cdot (0.4)^5\). The graph shows the height to be 1.636 after 5 bounces \((n=5)\).

      This is consistent with the reasoning:
    </para>
          <list id="list-00005">
            <item>The height of the ball is decreasing by a factor of about
        \(\frac{2}{5}\) after each bounce. </item>
            <item>After the 4th bounce, the height difference is 6. It should decrease by
        \(\frac{2}{5}\) for each bounce after that. So, after 1 more bounce (the 5th bounce),
        it will decrease by a factor of about \(\frac{2}{5}\). The 5th bounce would be \(6 \cdot \frac{2}{5} \approx 2.4\). </item>
            <item>Rounding either 1.636 or 2.4 results in the answer 2. </item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
